Z||000000||85 gt fiero||11-04-2001||03:09 PM||||I work at a car wash in holland and i see fieros driving around all day i have my fiero in the front on the grass right next to the busies road in holland and i have worked there for 6 months and have never had any fieros come through. Do you guys go to the car washes where they wash it by hand and then send it through the wash or do you wash it yourself. Also when i had my 85 gt rally car out front for sale i had the whole crew from penzoil come by and check it out they were all on lunch and they couldnt get enough of it they thought the car kicked a$$. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>RALLY CAR, FULL ROLL CAGE, SKID PLATES AND SOON TO BE BRIGHT YELLOW||||reg||12
Z||000001||Cheever3000||11-04-2001||03:22 PM||||About 75% of the time I go to the place that has the sprayer wand and the foaming brush, and it costs $1 (US).  The other 25% is done at home by hand with car wash concentrate and a big sponge.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||avengador1||11-04-2001||03:22 PM||||I do mine by hand to avoid scratches.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||85 gt fiero||11-04-2001||03:55 PM||||the only problem i ecer had was when i got out to wash it myself and i turned the ignition off the steering wheel was locked in the turning position and it rode over the wheel guide and broke my antenna off. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>RALLY CAR, FULL ROLL CAGE, SKID PLATES AND SOON TO BE BRIGHT YELLOW||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fformula88||11-04-2001||09:32 PM||||I have never washed my Fiero anywhere besides my driveway.  I do like the do-it-yourself coin wash places with the wand and foaming brush in the cold weather, and haven't had any scratching problems with on my daily driver.  When its 35 degrees out, and the car is covered in salt, I think its better to take it to the wash and maybe risk a scratch than to let the salt eat away at the clear coat.  I'd take my Fiero in this situation too, except I never drive it in the winter when there is salt on the roads. <P>I have never taken a car thru an automatic wash.  Just don't trust them!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Nashco||11-04-2001||09:53 PM||||My paint is shot as it is anyway, and I got a free automatic wash the other day with a gas fill up.  I tried it out and found that automatic car washers weren't made for a car with contours like mine <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  It sucked especially behind the back window and under the wing, obviously, because the scrubber couldn't get in there.<P>I use the quickie wand wash, only because there's no way to do otherwise at my apartments.  When I lived at home, I always did it by hand, doing a full detail job simultaneously.  The satisfaction after having cleaned your car all day and seeing it that clean is awesome, and if you do it several times you end up getting all of the little crevices.  I love a clean car <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroKyle||11-05-2001||12:01 AM||||I usually do it in town at the quarter wash. Its cheaper and doesn't leave swirl marks or scratches in the paint like the automatic one does. Besides you can power wash the mud out of the fenders and do your compartments. I do it sometimes in the driveway, but I live on a dirt road and I have a black car. I wash the car, take it to town, and its already dirty! When the Road commision oils down the road or its a day after the rain where the dirt is compacted down without mud, if ya go slow enough and no other cars are on the road, the car will stay clean. But anyways.....<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0PgAgVMwT!1W9RUb20vChbvv6QaE!2K1LWrFB6SxTnTchZgvIygv7Y3YwXpVdD8GeNkWpurYfoQxvNRK6j6kEmOFLoIHavbIr/Kyle-85-GT.jpg">    <P>My Fast 'n Black Car<BR>Kyle - 85' GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fierowrecker||11-05-2001||08:48 AM||||When I had my 84 on the road, I used to try auto washes, but found the suspention hit the guide rails...<BR>The quarter car wash didn't pay off...<BR>So I do mine in the drive...<BR>Plus I get a much better job too!<P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">        <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||MinnGreenGT||11-05-2001||10:24 AM||||I generally prefer to wash it in the driveway.  But I do head over to the "quarter-wash" when it's really dirty or I'm in a hurry.  Also the power wash is nice for spraying out the engine when it gets grimey.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Old Lar||11-05-2001||11:29 AM||||I do mine by hand or in the wash yourself booth.  Like some of the others, the car is low and hits on the rails.  All the water shorts out something and makes my service engine light come on, until the car drys out.||||reg||1
Z||000010||thomas_l||11-05-2001||01:08 PM||||Due to local outdoor watering restrictions, I go to the full serve wash since there are no coin-ops within a reasonable distance. They do a good job and no damage to date. Since mine lives in the garage 99% of the time, I only need a wash every couple of months when the dust builds up.||||reg||1
Z||000011||LoW_KeY||11-05-2001||01:25 PM||||I usually do it by hand then use the chamois &lt;-- spelling??<P>but If its totally filthy and I'm away from home I usually go through the hand sprayers.. do it yourself.<P>Right before my paint job I blew off the A on my formula ;-)||||reg||10
Z||000012||Fierochic88||11-05-2001||04:11 PM||||99% of the time I wash ours at home, occassionally I will go to a do it yourself but I take my own sponges and stuff cause I don't wanna risk a scratch.<P>Jennifer<P>------------------<BR>IM Username:  FieroChic88<BR>Silver 1988 GT<BR>Silver 1986 2m4<BR>Cobalt Blue 85 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.personal.psu.edu/users/j/j/jjr161/Fierochic88Signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
